  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2017 tutorial on <em>SemiParSampleSel</em> shows clear early reuse in applied and methodological research: the article has 9 citations and 9 documented downstream works, with follow-on papers appearing in statistics, biostatistics, health, socio-economic planning, and political science. The record points to a niche but visible methodological contribution, especially for researchers working with sample selection in count-data settings.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Karol Wyszynski and Giampiero Marra\u2019s article is best understood as a hands-on methodological guide rather than a purely theoretical paper. It explains how to use the R package <em>SemiParSampleSel</em> for fitting selection models for count responses by penalized maximum likelihood, with attention to non-random sample selection and flexible covariate effects. That combination of software and workflow makes the paper immediately usable for applied statisticians and econometricians.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The impact record shows that the tutorial has already been taken up by subsequent work, even if the overall citation base is still modest. OpenAlex records 9 citations and 9 downstream reuses, and the citing literature spans a mix of journal articles, a book, and a book chapter. The strongest pattern is disciplinary spread rather than volume: the article is cited in outlets such as <em>Computational Statistics &amp; Data Analysis</em>, <em>Statistical Methods in Medical Research</em>, <em>Socio-Economic Planning Sciences</em>, and <em>The International Journal of Health Planning and Management</em>, suggesting that the package and model class have been relevant across several applied domains.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing works also show that the paper\u2019s methodological framing has traveled into related areas of sample selection modeling, copula-based approaches, and econometric sensitivity analysis. Recent citations include work on bivariate discrete responses, treatment adherence and viral load in HIV research, and sample-selection bias in lending, alongside earlier methodological extensions in generalized additive and Heckman-type models. This pattern indicates that the article functions as a practical reference point for researchers adapting selection models to new data structures and application settings.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Evidence of broader attention is present but limited. Scite indexes six mentions, and the article has one documented dataset citation, showing some degree of formal reuse and methodological anchoring. OpenAIRE also records hybrid open access with multiple repository instances, which may have helped accessibility. Overall, the record suggests a focused but meaningful uptake: a software-oriented tutorial that has been incorporated into later methodological and applied research, with the strongest signal coming from reuse across adjacent quantitative fields.</p>",
